WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.960
Hi, it’s official, the Google Brain Team joined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:05.520
the pretrained vision and&nbsp;
language models business.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:08.320
Their new model called SimVLM uses a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:12.720
simple training procedure and&nbsp;
promises generative capabilities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:18.160
generalization, and zero-shot behavior when&nbsp;
it comes to tasks combining images and text

00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:23.360
so you can ask the model things by just&nbsp;
alluding to what can be seen in an image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:29.200
like “where to observe this animal” and the&nbsp;
model just answers to this: open-endedly!

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:33.600
If you’re curious about what the&nbsp;
authors say about how SimVLM works,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:38.320
about what they don’t say and how this compares&nbsp;
to previous vision and language models,&nbsp;

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:41.600
grab a cup of coffee and join&nbsp;
us for this AI Coffee Break!

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:48.160
We have known Google Research from&nbsp;
text-only models like T5, or vision&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:56.000
only model like ViT, but now, with SimVLM,
they propose a “visual language model”. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:02.000
prior literature called these things “vision&nbsp;
&amp; language models”, but sure, they’re Google,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.560
they are allowed to be visionary, right?

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:08.320
If you are not yet familiar with vision and&nbsp;
language transformers and want to get the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:14.240
prerequisite knowledge for this video, make&nbsp;
sure to check out at least this one previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:19.520
video where we explain how this is done.
Or rather: was done, because this paper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:25.040
here criticizes that the first vision and&nbsp;
language models did not process the raw image,

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:30.320
but features extracted by an object-detection&nbsp;
backbone, like the Faster-RCNN.

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:33.440
And then of course the problem&nbsp;
is that the transformer sees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:38.240
the image through the looking glass&nbsp;
of that Faster-RCNN and you know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:44.160
if that looking glass is cracked or blurry,&nbsp;
that might bottleneck the whole procedure.

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:50.880
But, some of the latest architectures, like&nbsp;
CLIP or ViLT do process the image end-to-end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:55.840
but the authors of SimVLM decided&nbsp;
against highlighting this too much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:58.240
and rather do not mention this at all.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:05.440
In any case, SimVLM gets rid of the Faster-RCNN&nbsp;
backbone and looks at the image like ViT does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:09.600
or almost: because they find&nbsp;
that the linear projection of ViT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:15.840
should be rather replaced by three blocks&nbsp;
of ResNet to extract image patch vectors.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:20.080
So sure, unlike most of the previous&nbsp;
work on vision and language models,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:23.280
these weights are learned and updated.

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:29.120
The text is tokenized and represented through&nbsp;
word vectors. And the image vectors and text&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:34.640
vectors alike, all endowed with positional&nbsp;
embeddings are blended and mixed together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:39.120
by a transformer, like they wouldn’t&nbsp;
even be part of different modalities.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:44.240
But remember, the only difference to previous work&nbsp;
is that the image vectors are now extracted by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:50.480
a ResNet that trains with the whole transformer,&nbsp;
while the original vision and language models, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:57.760
not the most recent ones, used a whole pretrained,&nbsp;
but then fixed, Faster-RCNN backbone for this.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:01.680
Okay, cool. Moving on to the&nbsp;
next contribution of the paper:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:05.840
The authors further highlight&nbsp;
shortcomings of existing work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:10.480
in that it uses multi-task learning&nbsp;
by formulating multiple losses,&nbsp;

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:14.080
like masked language modelling where&nbsp;
words are masked and the model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:18.880
now informed also with the image has to&nbsp;
predict what the masked word should be,&nbsp;

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:24.160
or the task of image-sentence alignment,
or masked region classification, and others.

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:30.000
But SimVLM does not need all this: it can get&nbsp;
away with one single objective, which is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:36.480
so-called Prefix Language Modelling objective.
Most vision and language models out there use the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:42.000
masked language modelling loss, where a BERT-based&nbsp;
vision and language transformer processes text&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:47.920
following the idea of a denoising autoencoder:
The original sequence is altered by randomly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:53.520
masking some tokens and the model must predict&nbsp;
what these mask tokens had originally contained.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:57.120
But this masked-language-modelling&nbsp;
based models are not really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:01.120
language models in the classical sense
because they can’t really compute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:08.320
the joint probability of a sentence by&nbsp;
autoregressively factorizing the probability.

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:12.400
You know, that is when you are&nbsp;
applying the chain rule over and over.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:17.680
And SimVLM wants to be like a language model&nbsp;
because they have the advantage that they can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:23.200
open-endedly generate text while they rely on&nbsp;
context and not on finetuning for zero-shot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:29.680
capabilities, as seen for example with GPT-3,
where one can show some examples as context at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:34.800
the start of the sequence and GPT-3 can&nbsp;
continue it by picking up the pattern.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:40.080
But now SimVLM wants to generate text&nbsp;
also given image tokens as context.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:45.200
So the authors use something similar to the&nbsp;
autoregressive modelling the GPT family does:&nbsp;

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:50.400
prefix language modelling, where the&nbsp;
autoregressive factorization does not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:53.600
happen on the whole sequence,&nbsp;
but only at the end of it.

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:57.200
So the image for example,&nbsp;
acts as a prefix or context,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:01.600
and then the autoregressive factorization&nbsp;
from left to right, happens only on the text.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:05.280
Of course, this prefix can extend on&nbsp;
the text tokens too if the goal is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:09.520
for the model to continue a piece of&nbsp;
text that has been already written.

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:15.680
With this, SimVLM processes the prefix much like&nbsp;
a masked language model, bidirectionally, but can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.680 --> 00:05:23.040
perform unidirectional open-ended text generation&nbsp;
on the rest, like a “real” language model.

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:27.200
And this is cool because this is how SimVLM can do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:32.800
things like this and complete a sequence of an&nbsp;
image and a question by answering that question.

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:37.760
What I understand so far from the paper is that&nbsp;
this autoregressiveness delivers these great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:44.640
zero shot abilities seen here, because SimVLM&nbsp;
is like GPT-3, but now with text and image.

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:51.920
But Ms. Coffee Bean, this makes no sense, because&nbsp;
GPT-2 is autoregressive in the same way GPT-3&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:58.720
is and while GPT-3 has zero-shot and few-shot&nbsp;
capabilities, GPT-2 doesn’t really have these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.720 --> 00:06:05.360
impressive features. Then what is the secret&nbsp;
ingredient here for SimVLM? What did we miss?

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:08.880
Let’s take a step back: what we&nbsp;
understand from this paper so far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:14.720
is that: all you need (sorry for this, I&nbsp;
actually cannot hear “all you need” anymore):&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:18.960
So all you need but wish you did not need,&nbsp;
is a vision and language transformer that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:23.680
processes the image without a huge and&nbsp;
stiff preprocessing step beforehand&nbsp;

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:27.360
and a model that is generative,&nbsp;
at least on the text part.

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:33.120
With this, SimVLM beats state of the art on&nbsp;
standard vision and language tasks like VQA,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.120 --> 00:06:36.800
NLVR2, SNLI-VE, image captioning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:42.240
Wow, right? It makes us wonder why nobody&nbsp;
came up with this simple idea before!&nbsp;

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:46.800
Ms. Coffee Bean, why are you&nbsp;
stopping me? Did I miss something?

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:52.000
Oh yes, I did. The data. I mean of&nbsp;
course, SimVLM must be pre-trained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.000 --> 00:06:56.400
on lots and lots of data first, like&nbsp;
every other model beforehand too.

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:03.920
So this data, is, we cite: a “large-scale&nbsp;
weakly labelled dataset, which has better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:09.600
potential towards zero-shot generalization”.&nbsp;
What does this last part mean? I don’t know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:16.560
let’s go to the dedicated data section ...
So, they are using, we cite: a “large-scale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:21.360
noisy image-text data, which has better&nbsp;
potential for zero-shot generalization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:27.520
Specifically, we use the image and alt-text&nbsp;
pairs introduced in Jia et al. (2021),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:31.600
which are crawled from the web&nbsp;
with minimal post-processing.”.

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:38.000
Ah, so these are images and captions.
Certainly not like the 330,000 MSCOCO&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:45.440
image-text pairs, because MSCOCO has images&nbsp;
and well-written crowd-sourced captions,&nbsp;

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:50.320
maybe much more like the 3.3M sized Conceptual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:55.120
Captions dataset that contains images&nbsp;
and alt-text captions, but curated.&nbsp;

00:07:55.120 --> 00:08:00.560
Or maybe something as large-scale as OpenAI’s&nbsp;
crawled image and alt-text captions they used for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:05.920
CLIP, right? So, this simple architecture&nbsp;
of SimVLM and this data section completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:10.880
explain the superior results of SimVLM&nbsp;
over everything else (they compare to).

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:16.640
Ms. Coffee Bean, why are you poking me? You&nbsp;
tell me that I should look for the amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:20.560
data that this mysterious Jia and&nbsp;
collaborators dataset contains?

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:23.920
Oh, ok, sure. I mean, sure,&nbsp;
it will be something similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:25.840
to these numbers of the related work, right?

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:30.160
WHAAT, you are telling me&nbsp;
that we are speaking about&nbsp;

00:08:30.160 --> 00:08:33.760
over 1 BILLION image-text pairs?

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:40.080
But Ms. Coffee Bean, the number billion,&nbsp;
or any number indicating the amount of data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:45.040
samples is not anywhere in the SimVLM&nbsp;
paper, I had to check another one!?&nbsp;

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:47.440
And “large-scale” does not necessarily mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:53.120
1 billion, hundreds of million were still&nbsp;
considered large-scale the last I checked.

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:58.000
Ok. If the amount of used data is not a&nbsp;
thing to put in the abstract and everywhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:03.200
else in the paper, I don’t know what is. So&nbsp;
you’re telling me that they are training on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:07.600
one billion data samples, and beat&nbsp;
the state of the art but don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.600 --> 00:09:13.520
even mention the data amount and advertise&nbsp;
the architectural simplicity of end-to-end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:16.880
image processing and that they&nbsp;
need just one loss, instead?

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:22.240
But it is exactly this, the data aspect,&nbsp;
this is the clue to everything! We need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:27.200
to start from the billion images and&nbsp;
then explain what we can do with that.

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:33.440
If we have 10,000 times more data samples to go&nbsp;
with than LXMERT had for pretraining, then of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:39.040
course we do not need as many losses and the heavy&nbsp;
multi-task learning procedure that LXMERT needs!

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:45.680
LXMERT has to compensate the lack of data&nbsp;
with more thorough supervision, so losses!

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:49.360
If we have 1000 times more data&nbsp;
than ViLBERT had during pretraining,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.360 --> 00:09:55.920
then of course we have more freedom to learn our&nbsp;
own image representations instead of employing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.920 --> 00:10:02.480
the inductive biases of other architectures like&nbsp;
the faster-rcnn and not even train this further!

00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:05.600
Ms. Coffee Bean, get me out of here. I’m too mad.&nbsp;

00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:11.200
What I meant to say by this: I hope to see a&nbsp;
better framing of the advances of SimVLM in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:18.080
future version of this paper. I hope to see more&nbsp;
emphasis on the enormous importance of the data.

00:10:18.080 --> 00:10:23.120
Choosing architectural details, inserting,&nbsp;
or getting rid of inductive biases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.840 --> 00:10:28.960
heavily relies on the amount of available&nbsp;
training data. It’s not like nobody before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:33.360
reading this paper never thought about&nbsp;
removing the faster-rcnn backbone or getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:38.160
rid of all the losses making optimization overly&nbsp;
complicated in most vision and language models.

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:45.760
But the authors of SimVLM thought about this&nbsp;
AND could afford doing this and did all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:52.160
heavy lifting to get all the tiny details right&nbsp;
so that this beast can train in parallel over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.160 --> 00:10:59.040
many GPUs -- and converge. They’ve done a&nbsp;
great job and have written a great paper!

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:05.120
It becomes clearer and clearer how hard it is&nbsp;
to keep making good science: In a scientific&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:10.000
experiment, in order to show the gains of a&nbsp;
component with respect to the state-of-the-art,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.000 --> 00:11:16.240
that component and that component only should&nbsp;
be changed and the rest should be kept fixed.

00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:21.040
In this case, it is nice to see this simpler&nbsp;
architecture with no object detection backbone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.040 --> 00:11:25.920
and the simplified training procedure,&nbsp;
but this is also combined with bringing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:31.280
up a lot more data than the related work&nbsp;
had and we do not see at all how these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:36.560
choices behave in the smaller data&nbsp;
regimes previous architectures had.

00:11:36.560 --> 00:11:42.000
Especially because nobody else has the 1&nbsp;
Billion image-text dataset the authors here have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:46.560
to conduct the missing experiments showing&nbsp;
how the proposed architecture behaves with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:52.000
respect to increasing amounts of data,&nbsp;
it is even more the author’s job to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:57.600
conduct these comparisons and show where the&nbsp;
performance gains in SimVLM really come from.

00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:00.960
So I’m just calling for more&nbsp;
honesty when highlighting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:04.880
paper contributions and where&nbsp;
the gains come from. That’s all.

00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:11.680
In any case, it’s an arxiv version of the paper&nbsp;
anyway where maybe getting a first version out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:17.920
was a rush to mark the territory, who knows?&nbsp;
I’m sure we will see an improved version soon.

00:12:17.920 --> 00:12:21.840
Thanks for listening until the&nbsp;
end, even if it got really ranty.&nbsp;&nbsp;

